PAG refinement in C465 was far less sensitive to heating rate than 300 M, as can be seen from the relatively consistent PAG size across all heating rates in C465 after holding at 982 °C for 1 hour [Figure  16 (a)]. This suggests that recovery over the temperature range between  Ac 1  and the start of recrystallisation was insufficient to produce a difference in recrystallisation behaviour over the range of heating rates investigated. It may also be inferred that the mechanism for recrystallisation in this alloy may have been consistent over the range of heating rates investigated, and may have been similar to that observed in PH 13-8 Mo by Rosenauer  et al . [ 18 ]  It is likely that in C465 recrystallisation occurred primarily through the strain induced migration of pre-existing high angle grain boundaries, similar to the mechanism observed in 300 M at the 0.1 to 0.2 °C s −1  heating rates and in the Test 1  in-situ  experiment. This can be inferred from the fact that recrystallised C465 developed a texture with the  001 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
